 ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
 :blocked-layers
 {:as-float
  {:status :blocked
   :probe "q9-probes/g23-f64.kotoba"
   :check-exit 0
   :native-compile-exit 70
   :refusal "typed values currently require the kotoba-script web target, typed Wasm target, or qualified native string/scalar-record/option-i64/result-i64 features"
   :reached-by-this-component true
   :why-that-matters
   "This is the one place com-aadhaar is materially worse off than the com-aave
    sibling, and it must not be read across from that record. aave coerces
    NOTHING to :float, so its missing as-float was a public function of the
    oracle that no code path reached. aadhaar's Transaction spec is
    :coerce {:amount :float} and `amount` is ALSO one of Transaction's two
    required fields, so the blocked arm sits on the create and update path of
    one of the six entities."
   :measured-divergence
   "coerce-field on the :float kind, oracle vs port, five inputs:
      JSON number 12.5    -> 12.5 vs 12.5    agree
      JSON string \"12.5\" -> 12.5 vs \"12.5\" DIFFER
      JSON string \"abc\"  -> 0    vs \"abc\"  DIFFER
      JSON number 0       -> 0    vs 0       agree
      absent field        -> 0    vs null    DIFFER
    Three of five disagree. The port keeps the raw JSON token where the oracle
    stores a double."
   :not-reshaped
   "The entity table still declares amount:float truthfully and coerce-field
    carries an explicit `float` arm rather than letting the case fall through
    :else, so the divergence is visible in the source instead of being an
    accident of cond ordering."
   :not-proven-to-be-a-language-gap
   "Only aarch64-macos was compiled. The refusal names kotoba-script web and
    typed Wasm as targets that DO carry typed values, so this may be a gap in
    THIS TARGET rather than in the language. Not tried."}}

