 :language-subset-gaps-measured-20260903
 ;; KEPT VERBATIM, because what it got wrong is more instructive than what it
 ;; got right. Superseded by :language-subset-gaps-remeasured-20260909 below.
 {:superseded-by :language-subset-gaps-remeasured-20260909
  :as-of "2026-09-03"
  :amu "57ba0ee0"
  :command "amu check --jvm-free"
  :claim
  "PASS: (get m :k) (get m :k 0) (assoc m :k 1) {:a x} [x x] (case x 1 2 0)
         (if b 1 2) (pos? x) (empty?) (first) (take 2 xs) map/vector literals,
         int arithmetic.
   REJECTED (:subset-reject / :source-read-failed):
     string literals anywhere (def or fn body); (str a b) / (str a) / (str x \"y\");
     parse-long / lower-case / trim; (count v); (reduce f 0 xs); (mapv f xs);
     (filterv f xs); (remove f xs); (min a b); (seq xs); (some-> x f);
     (keys m); (reduce-kv f 0 m); (long x); #() fn literals (read failure);
     (:k m) keyword projection (:record-projection-unresolved);
     (contains? m :k) on maps (:map-presence-receiver);
     (def d [{:a 1} {:b 2}]) vector-of-maps top-level constant."
  :original-blocked-reason
  "The whole public surface of aadhaar.main cannot be expressed in the admitted
   kotoba subset exposed by amu --jvm-free at amu@57ba0ee0: every route spec,
   error message, id prefix, filter comparison and entity name is a string;
   handlers fold with reduce/mapv/filterv/reduce-kv/keys. Shrinking the
   component to the int-only decision core is forbidden
   (:decision-only-extraction-forbidden), so the whole-component migration is
   recorded as :blocked, not :partial."
  :verdict-on-that-reason
  "False in its premise and therefore false in its conclusion. Strings, count,
   reduce, mapv, filterv, min and #() literals all pass. The component was
   built without shrinking it."}

 :language-subset-gaps-remeasured-20260909
 {:as-of "2026-09-09"
  :amu "6ffc1d71"
  :command "amu check --jvm-free"
  :probes "q9-probes/ (one file per row; failing probes kept as evidence)"
  :rows-claimed-rejected 18
  :rows-that-reproduce 2

  :passes
  ["string literals anywhere" "(str a b)" "(count v)" "(reduce f 0 xs)"
   "(mapv f xs)" "(filterv f xs)" "(min a b)" "#() fn literals"
   "typed-map-get (previously UNRESOLVED)" "reduce (previously UNRESOLVED)"]

  :vocabulary-mismatch
  ["parse-long" "lower-case" "(remove f xs)" "(seq xs)" "(some-> x f)"
   "(keys m)" "(reduce-kv f 0 m)" "(long x)" "(:k m)" "(contains? m :k)"]

  :reproduces
  ["trim" "(def d [{:a 1} {:b 2}]) as a USED top-level constant"]

  :trim
  {:message "unknown operation: trim is not a builtin, a sugar head, or a function of this module"
   :assessment
   "A stdlib gap, not a language limit. No admitted head trims under any
    spelling, but a hand-written walk over string-code-point-at and
    string-substring passes and is what this component uses. It did not block
    the port."}

  :vector-of-maps-constant
  {:message "map literal with keyword keys carries i64 values; the value at key :entity is :string. A keyword-keyed literal lowers to the legacy untagged pair map that `map-get`, `map-assoc` and every `match` map pattern are written against, so its value type is not inferred; write (typed-map-new [:map :keyword :string] ...) for a typed map with keyword keys"
   :isolated
   "q9-probes/g20k-i64-count-control.kotoba is the same def with only the
    ELEMENT TYPE changed, same consumer, and it passes. So the refusal is the
    map element, not the vector constant."
   :no-native-workaround
   "(document [...]) inside a function body passes check, and then fails
    compilation for aarch64-macos with exit 70. For the native target there is
    no working spelling of a vector-of-maps constant. This is precisely why the
    component carries its entity table as comma-separated strings."
   :was-recorded-pass-in-the-previous-round
   "and that PASS was a measurement defect -- see :the-method-error below."}

  :the-method-error
  "Three distinct causes, and the first is the one worth carrying forward.

   1. UNUSED DEFINITIONS ARE NOT TYPE-CHECKED. The identical
      (def specs [{:a 1} {:a 2}]) answers exit 0 when nothing consumes it and
      is refused with 'expression type mismatch: expected i64, got map' when an
      exported function's return value reaches it. The passing run does not even
      list `specs` in its :order. That is exactly how the vector-of-maps row was
      recorded PASS on 2026-09-03 while actually being refused. Every probe in
      this round consumes its own result.

   2. CLOJURE'S VOCABULARY TESTED AGAINST A DIFFERENT LANGUAGE. parse-long,
      lower-case, seq, remove, reduce-kv and long are not heads; the
      capabilities exist under other names. A probe in Clojure's spelling
      measures whether Clojure's names resolve, not whether the capability
      exists.

   3. ROWS THAT WERE NEVER GAPS. str, mapv and filterv are accepted as spelled
      in Clojure AND are the same operations as string-concat, map and filter --
      proven by identical definition CIDs, not by inspection.

   A :blocked disposition derived this way understates the language and, left
   standing, keeps a component out of the migration for a reason that was never
   true. This one stood for six days."

  :the-limit-of-a-check-only-table
  "Every verdict in this section is `amu check --jvm-free`, the FRONTEND ONLY.
   Check acceptance is NOT backend acceptance, and this component proves it
   twice: its blocked :f64 layer passes check and is refused by aarch64 with
   exit 70, and so is the only workaround for the vector-of-maps row. A gap
   table measured with check alone cannot say what will run. The 2026-09-03
   table was measured that way and so is this one; the difference is that this
   one says so."}

